example.paragraph:
Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1.8 & .8 \\
      .2 & 1.2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\textbf{v}_{1} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\textbf{x} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- .5} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Then $A$ has eigenvalues $2~$ and $1$, and the eigenspace for $\text{λ}_{1} = 2$ is the line through $\textbf{0}$ and $\textbf{v}_{1}$. For $k = 0,\ldots,8$, compute $A^{k}\textbf{x}$ and construct the line through $\textbf{0}$ and $A^{k}\textbf{x}$. What happens as $k$ increases?

statement.paragraph:
Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {- 2} & {- 3} \\
      6 & 7
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Repeat Exercise 5, using the following sequence $\textbf{x},A\textbf{x},\ldots,A^{5}\textbf{x}$.$$\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\mspace{-4mu},\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 5} \\
      13
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\mspace{-4mu},\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 29} \\
      61
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\mspace{-4mu},\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 125} \\
      253
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\mspace{-4mu},\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 509} \\
      1021
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\mspace{-4mu},\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2045} \\
      4093
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$$
